1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:04,720
In 2003, a small bank from
Stellenbosch had about 25,000

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:10,160
clients. Today it has 26.6 million.

3
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:12,640
That's more than any other
bank in South Africa,

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:15,600
and about twice as many
as the next biggest one.

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,680
That bank is Capitec.

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:21,440
It started as a small money
lender, up against giants.

7
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:26,160
So how did it win? In this video,
you'll get the story in plain words,

8
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:30,560
what it actually did differently,
and the lesson for your own pocket:

9
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:35,920
how to work out what your bank really
costs you each month, and how to pay less.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,680
Let's start from zero.

11
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,240
Our story starts in the year 2000,
with a company called PSG,

12
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:44,160
founded by Jannie Mouton.

13
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:46,840
PSG owned a microlending business.

14
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,360
That means small, short
loans to ordinary people.

15
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,600
It had bought two lenders,
SmartFin and FinAid,

16
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,613
with 68 small branches between them.

17
00:00:56,747 --> 00:01:00,400
Then PSG decided to turn
that business into a bank.

18
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,493
Michiel le Roux became
the first chief executive.

19
00:01:03,547 --> 00:01:07,227
He brought in experienced bankers,
including Riaan Stassen.

20
00:01:07,253 --> 00:01:09,960
And a banker called Gerrie
Fourie joined them too.

21
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,147
Many years later, he would
run the whole company.

22
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:18,040
On the first of March 2001,
Capitec was founded, in Stellenbosch.

23
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:19,760
That was a brave move.

24
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,880
Lending people money is one thing.

25
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:27,000
Asking them to trust you with their salary,
every single month, is something else.

26
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:29,080
Now, here's the important part.

27
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,893
Who was Capitec for? The big banks
already had the wealthier clients.

28
00:01:33,947 --> 00:01:37,973
So Capitec went after a different
market: lower-income workers,

29
00:01:38,027 --> 00:01:40,960
and people who'd never
had a bank account at all.

30
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:45,893
Capitec describes its idea in one
sentence: banking should be simple,

31
00:01:46,187 --> 00:01:47,840
transparent and affordable.

32
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:51,600
Simple means one main account,
not a menu of twenty.

33
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,280
Transparent means you
can see what you pay.

34
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,907
And affordable means low fees.

35
00:01:57,093 --> 00:02:00,520
Today, Capitec's monthly
account fee is R7.50.

36
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:03,400
It sounds obvious. But it was a big bet.

37
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,800
When each client pays only a few rand,

38
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,560
a bank only makes real money
with a huge number of clients.

39
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,240
And the clients came.

40
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,760
From about 25,000 clients in 2003,

41
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,573
Capitec grew to 24.1
million by February 2025,

42
00:02:19,627 --> 00:02:23,240
and to 26.6 million by August this year.

43
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,800
That's more than a thousand times bigger.

44
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:30,240
Branches grew from 266 to 885.

45
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:35,040
And profit followed. Capitec's headline
earnings, a main measure of profit,

46
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,720
were R30 million in 2003.

47
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:43,520
In the year to February 2026,
they were R16.8 billion.

48
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:48,640
And in just the six months to
August this year, R9.5 billion,

49
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:53,120
up 19%. The founders
stepped back over the years.

50
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,800
Gerrie Fourie, the last founder
running the company day to day,

51
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:02,320
retired in July 2025, and Graham
Lee took over as chief executive.

52
00:03:03,186 --> 00:03:06,786
So how does a bank with low
fees make that much money?

53
00:03:07,106 --> 00:03:10,706
First, loans. When a bank lends you money,

54
00:03:10,746 --> 00:03:13,426
the interest you pay is the bank's income.

55
00:03:13,746 --> 00:03:18,786
Capitec started as a lender, and lending
is still a big part of the business.

56
00:03:18,866 --> 00:03:24,186
Remember that whenever a bank offers
you credit: your loan is their income.

57
00:03:24,546 --> 00:03:27,506
Second, the money you
keep in your account.

58
00:03:27,586 --> 00:03:29,719
The bank pays you a little interest on it,

59
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:32,706
and lends that same money
out at a higher rate.

60
00:03:32,786 --> 00:03:34,706
The difference is profit.

61
00:03:34,906 --> 00:03:38,066
Third, fees. Each fee is small,

62
00:03:38,146 --> 00:03:43,746
but multiply a few rand by millions of
clients, every month, and it adds up fast.

63
00:03:43,826 --> 00:03:45,986
Fourth, new businesses.

64
00:03:46,106 --> 00:03:50,386
Capitec now sells cellphone airtime
and data through Capitec Connect,

65
00:03:50,466 --> 00:03:54,866
which it calls the country's largest
mobile virtual network operator.

66
00:03:54,986 --> 00:03:59,106
And 16.5 million people
use its banking app.

67
00:03:59,186 --> 00:04:01,506
Three things stand out in this story.

68
00:04:01,586 --> 00:04:04,146
It served people others overlooked.

69
00:04:04,226 --> 00:04:05,986
It kept prices simple.

70
00:04:06,026 --> 00:04:11,106
And it pushed hard into the app, so millions
of clients can bank without a branch.

71
00:04:11,186 --> 00:04:16,279
Quick note: this isn't a recommendation
to bank with Capitec, or with anyone else.

72
00:04:16,333 --> 00:04:19,506
The lesson is about you,
not about one bank.

73
00:04:19,666 --> 00:04:21,906
Because here's what most people miss.

74
00:04:22,026 --> 00:04:25,146
Your bank fees are not
just the monthly fee.

75
00:04:25,186 --> 00:04:27,639
Every bank charges in two ways.

76
00:04:27,693 --> 00:04:31,959
A fixed monthly fee, and then
a price for each thing you do.

77
00:04:32,013 --> 00:04:34,093
A debit order. Drawing cash.

78
00:04:34,119 --> 00:04:36,506
Paying someone. An instant payment.

79
00:04:36,546 --> 00:04:39,626
Many people compare banks
by the monthly fee alone,

80
00:04:39,666 --> 00:04:42,199
and then lose more on the small things.

81
00:04:42,253 --> 00:04:44,106
Think of a prepaid cellphone.

82
00:04:44,146 --> 00:04:48,813
The price per minute looks small,
but your bill depends on how much you use.

83
00:04:48,919 --> 00:04:50,679
Banking works the same way.

84
00:04:50,733 --> 00:04:54,466
The truth is, what you pay
depends on how you bank.

85
00:04:54,546 --> 00:04:58,546
Let's take Lerato. We'll
use Capitec's 2026 prices,

86
00:04:58,626 --> 00:05:01,146
simply because they're
published and easy to follow.

87
00:05:01,186 --> 00:05:03,146
Your bank's prices will be different.

88
00:05:03,186 --> 00:05:05,386
Lerato's month looks like this.

89
00:05:05,426 --> 00:05:08,013
The monthly fee: R7.50.

90
00:05:08,119 --> 00:05:11,786
Four debit orders, for insurance,
a phone contract and two others,

91
00:05:11,826 --> 00:05:16,906
at R3 each: R12. She draws
R2,000 cash at an ATM,

92
00:05:16,946 --> 00:05:20,106
at R10 per R1,000: R20.

93
00:05:20,146 --> 00:05:25,066
She pays friends at the same
bank five times, at R1 each: R5.

94
00:05:25,106 --> 00:05:29,066
Two normal payments to
other banks, at R2 each: R4.

95
00:05:29,106 --> 00:05:32,906
And two immediate payments,
at R6 each: R12.

96
00:05:32,946 --> 00:05:35,719
Add it up: R60.50 a month.

97
00:05:35,773 --> 00:05:40,746
That's R726 a year.
Now Lerato changes two habits.

98
00:05:40,786 --> 00:05:44,266
She pays with her card in shops,
where card purchases are free,

99
00:05:44,306 --> 00:05:45,666
instead of drawing cash.

100
00:05:45,746 --> 00:05:49,506
And she pays people with normal
payments instead of immediate ones.

101
00:05:49,986 --> 00:05:55,666
Her new total: R32.50 a
month, or R390 a year.

102
00:05:56,226 --> 00:05:58,106
Same bank, same life.

103
00:05:58,386 --> 00:06:03,906
She saves R336 a year, just by
changing how she uses her account.

104
00:06:04,386 --> 00:06:06,786
And remember, those are low fees.

105
00:06:07,186 --> 00:06:10,066
If your bank charges more
for each transaction,

106
00:06:10,146 --> 00:06:12,866
the same habits can cost you a lot more.

107
00:06:13,917 --> 00:06:16,357
Now, the five fee traps.

108
00:06:16,637 --> 00:06:19,837
Trap one: drawing cash you don't need.

109
00:06:19,957 --> 00:06:23,997
Each withdrawal costs money,
and cash is easier to spend.

110
00:06:24,157 --> 00:06:26,637
Trap two: paying for speed.

111
00:06:26,677 --> 00:06:29,677
An immediate payment costs
more than a normal one.

112
00:06:29,757 --> 00:06:32,477
Use it only when it really can't wait.

113
00:06:32,637 --> 00:06:35,437
Trap three: debit orders you forgot about.

114
00:06:35,517 --> 00:06:39,517
Old gym contracts, insurance
you don't use, subscriptions.

115
00:06:39,597 --> 00:06:42,397
Each one costs you the payment and a fee.

116
00:06:42,557 --> 00:06:46,077
Trap four: doing in a branch
what you could do on the app.

117
00:06:46,157 --> 00:06:50,877
At Capitec, for example, paying another
Capitec client costs R1 on the app,

118
00:06:50,957 --> 00:06:55,757
but R6 in a branch. Trap five:
ignoring small amounts.

119
00:06:55,837 --> 00:06:59,117
R3 here and R6 there feels like nothing.

120
00:06:59,157 --> 00:07:03,677
But Lerato's small fees
came to R726 a year.

121
00:07:03,757 --> 00:07:07,117
Here's how to check your
own bank fees, step by step.

122
00:07:07,237 --> 00:07:10,717
Step one. Download your
last three bank statements.

123
00:07:10,797 --> 00:07:13,437
Step two. Find every fee.

124
00:07:13,517 --> 00:07:16,877
Look for words like fee, charge or admin.

125
00:07:16,957 --> 00:07:21,597
Some banks list each fee line by line,
and some show a total at the bottom.

126
00:07:21,717 --> 00:07:24,677
Step three. Add them
up and divide by three.

127
00:07:24,717 --> 00:07:27,037
That's your real monthly bank cost.

128
00:07:27,117 --> 00:07:30,744
Step four. Find your bank's
pricing guide on its website.

129
00:07:30,770 --> 00:07:33,317
Banks usually update
their prices once a year.

130
00:07:33,357 --> 00:07:37,877
Capitec's 2026 prices, for example,
started on the first of March.

131
00:07:37,917 --> 00:07:41,517
Step five. Look at your
three biggest fees, and ask:

132
00:07:41,557 --> 00:07:43,757
can I change a habit to avoid them?

133
00:07:43,917 --> 00:07:46,757
Step six. Only then compare banks.

134
00:07:46,797 --> 00:07:49,557
Use your own habits,
not just the monthly fee.

135
00:07:49,597 --> 00:07:54,744
Let's recap. Capitec started in 2001
as a small lender in Stellenbosch.

136
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:58,770
It went after people the big banks
overlooked, kept its prices simple,

137
00:07:58,824 --> 00:08:03,810
and grew from about 25,000
clients to 26.6 million,

138
00:08:03,864 --> 00:08:06,117
the most of any bank in South Africa.

139
00:08:06,157 --> 00:08:11,330
And the lesson for you: your bank cost
is the monthly fee plus every small fee.

140
00:08:11,384 --> 00:08:16,050
Add up three months of statements, find
your biggest fees, and change one habit.

141
00:08:16,104 --> 00:08:19,597
For Lerato, that was R336 a year.

142
00:08:19,677 --> 00:08:22,984
This video is general information,
not financial advice.

143
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:25,557
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

144
00:08:25,597 --> 00:08:29,140
New Shorts every day, and a
full guide like this every week.

145
00:08:29,277 --> 00:08:30,477
See you in the next one.

